                        "review": "[h1]This game has already broken my heart once… which is why I bought it again.[/h1]\nThat's what I call [i]emotional self-care[/i] 😌\n\nQuick note: the game throws [i]a lot of[/i] text at you. [b]A LOT.[/b] So if you're the type who skips dialogue, never reads notes, and needs action to stay entertained — this one isn't for you.\n\nI first played it when I was a teenager. A lot of things felt and hit differently back then. Even so, I still remember finishing my first playthrough late at night, teary-eyed, overwhelmed with emotions I wasn't ready for.\n\nNow some of the stories and their outcomes feel overly dramatic and unnecessarily exaggerated to me, but that doesn't make them any less touching. They're still worth your time.\n\nEveryone in this game loves someone, and everyone loses someone. But each of them has their own kind of love — and to me, that's exactly what makes this game so compelling. It's made up of short episodes about characters whose lives intertwine and shape each other in one way or another. Even a brief, passing appearance of one character in another's life can lead to something far bigger than you'd expect. And at some point, you catch yourself following all these connections not as an observer, but as a participant. You're not just reading other people's stories — sometimes you see yourself in them. And that's what touches you the most.\n\nThe music in this game is genuinely beautiful (especially in the main menu!!). It sets the atmosphere and complements the stories perfectly. Every time Jimmy's storyline came up, the music alone made me smile and wonder: [i]What is it this time, Jimmy…[/i]\n\nThe story of Li Wen and Wen Zhaoren is still my absolute favorite. They're one of the reasons this game is so special to me. On that first run, it really got to me. I just wanted those two to be happy. Years later, replaying the game, I finally realized they [i]were[/i] happy — just not the way I imagined back then. And that's probably the main thing this game taught me: sometimes just being there means more than anything. Not everything has to go the way we want. Some things can't be changed or fixed. And at some point, you just have to accept the way things ended and move on.\n\nThe game is truly [i]wonderful[/i], but unfortunately, it has its flaws too. As I mentioned earlier, some plot twists feel excessive and more like [i]drama for drama's sake[/i]. I've played it in both Russian and English, and I have to say, the translation gives me mixed feelings. In both versions, the text sounds natural and flows well in some places, but in others it feels downright awkward and, at times, ridiculous. Some lines make you wince — and that ruins the whole atmosphere. To be fair, maybe the issue is the original text rather than the translation. Often you're looking at a set of sentences, you know they need to be rearranged, but you have no idea why [i]this[/i] particular order should work. It feels like you could shuffle them forever and get the same result. And in those moments, instead of feeling like [i]I'm a god rewriting destinies[/i], you feel like [i]I'm a toddler smashing buttons and hoping something happens[/i]. This gets especially frustrating late in the game, when there are already plenty of stories. You've got three letters open at once, and all you can do is pray to the [i]Goddess of Fortune[/i] and trust your intuition.\n\nDespite it all, I don't regret a single minute I spent with this game. Most of what I felt comes from my first playthrough. Now I see things a little differently. I notice flaws that never bothered me before. But none of that changes how fond I am of it. I still remember sitting in that heavy silence after the credits, processing the whole story. I think that says more about whether it was worth it than any words could. Not a [i]Recommended[/i] — just the fact that I'm writing this review many years later. Thanks so much, [i]Will: A Wonderful World[/i], for the memories and for this painful but beautiful story.❤️‍🩹",
